<TMU|<tuple|1.1.0|2026.2.7-rc6>>

<style|<tuple|generic|number-europe|preview-ref>>

<\body>
  <\hide-preamble>
    <assign|me|<macro|<math-up|e>>>% 自然指数 <assign|dif|<macro|<math-up|d>>>% 微分 d

    <assign|imag|<macro|<math-up|i>>>% 虚数单位

    <assign|bra|<macro|1|\<langle\><arg|1>\|>><assign|ket|<macro|1|\|<arg|1>\<rangle\>>><assign|braket|<macro|1|2|\<langle\><arg|1>\|<arg|2>\<rangle\>>>
  </hide-preamble>

  <doc-data|<doc-title|Solution of the Schrödinger Equation for the Hydrogen Atom>>

  <section|Two-body problem and separation of the centre of mass>

  The hydrogen atom consists of a proton of mass <math|m<rsub|p>> and an electron of mass <math|m<rsub|e>> interacting via the Coulomb potential

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:V>V<around|(|r|)>=-<frac|e<rsup|2>|4*\<pi\>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*<space|0.17em>r>,
  </equation>

  where <math|e> is the elementary charge and <math|r> the distance between the two particles. The total time-independent Schr"odinger equation reads

  <\equation>
    <around*|[|-<frac|\<hbar\><rsup|2>|2*m<rsub|p>>*\<nabla\><rsup|2><rsub|p>-<frac|\<hbar\><rsup|2>|2*m<rsub|e>>*\<nabla\><rsup|2><rsub|e>+V<around|(|<around|\||<math-bf|r><rsub|p>-<math-bf|r><rsub|e>|\|>|)>|]>*\<Psi\><around|(|<math-bf|r><rsub|p>,<math-bf|r><rsub|e>|)>=E<rsub|tot>*\<Psi\><around|(|<math-bf|r><rsub|p>,<math-bf|r><rsub|e>|)>.
  </equation>

  Introduce the centre-of-mass coordinate <math|<math-bf|R>> and the relative coordinate <math|<math-bf|r>>,

  <\equation*>
    <math-bf|R>=<frac|m<rsub|p><math-bf|r><rsub|p>+m<rsub|e><math-bf|r><rsub|e>|m<rsub|p>+m<rsub|e>>,<space|2em><math-bf|r>=<math-bf|r><rsub|e>-<math-bf|r><rsub|p>,
  </equation*>

  together with the total mass <math|M=m<rsub|p>+m<rsub|e>> and the reduced mass\ 

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\>=<frac|m<rsub|p>*m<rsub|e>|m<rsub|p>+m<rsub|e>>.
  </equation*>

  The Hamiltonian separates into a free-particle part for <math|<math-bf|R>> and an internal part for <math|<math-bf|r>>:\ 

  <\equation*>
    H=-<frac|\<hbar\><rsup|2>|2*M>*\<nabla\><rsup|2><rsub|<math-bf|R>><space|0.27em>-<space|0.27em><frac|\<hbar\><rsup|2>|2*\<mu\>>*\<nabla\><rsup|2><rsub|<math-bf|r>>+V<around|(|r|)>.
  </equation*>

  Writing <math|\<Psi\><around|(|<math-bf|r><rsub|p>,<math-bf|r><rsub|e>|)>=\<Phi\><around|(|<math-bf|R>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<psi\><around|(|<math-bf|r>|)>> yields the internal Schr"odinger equation

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:internalSE><boxed|-<frac|\<hbar\><rsup|2>|2*\<mu\>>*\<nabla\><rsup|2>*\<psi\><around|(|<math-bf|r>|)>-<frac|e<rsup|2>|4*\<pi\>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*r>*<space|0.17em>\<psi\><around|(|<math-bf|r>|)>=E*<space|0.17em>\<psi\><around|(|<math-bf|r>|)>>,
  </equation>

  where <math|E> is the energy of the relative motion (the bound-state energies we are interested in). In the following we use the shorthand <math|V<around|(|r|)>=-<dfrac|e<rsup|2>|4*\<pi\>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*r>>.

  <section|Spherical coordinates>

  Because the potential depends only on <math|r=<around|\||<math-bf|r>|\|>>, it is natural to work in spherical coordinates <math|<around|(|r,\<theta\>,\<phi\>|)>>. The Laplacian reads

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:Laplacian>\<nabla\><rsup|2>=<frac|1|r<rsup|2>>*<frac|\<partial\>|\<partial\>*r>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|r<rsup|2>*<frac|\<partial\>|\<partial\>*r>|)>+<frac|1|r<rsup|2>*sin \<theta\>>*<frac|\<partial\>|\<partial\>*\<theta\>><space|-0.17em><around*|(|sin \<theta\><frac|\<partial\>|\<partial\>*\<theta\>>|)>+<frac|1|r<rsup|2>*sin<rsup|2> \<theta\>>*<frac|\<partial\><rsup|2>|\<partial\>*\<phi\><rsup|2>>.
  </equation>

  We look for solutions of the form

  <\equation>
    \<psi\><around|(|r,\<theta\>,\<phi\>|)>=R<around|(|r|)>*<space|0.17em>Y<around|(|\<theta\>,\<phi\>|)>.
  </equation>

  Substituting into (<reference|eq:internalSE>) and multiplying by <math|r<rsup|2>/<around|(|R*Y|)>> we obtain

  <\equation*>
    <frac|1|R>*<frac|<dif>|<dif>r>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|r<rsup|2>*<frac|<dif>R|<dif>r>|)>+<frac|2*\<mu\>*r<rsup|2>|\<hbar\><rsup|2>>*<around*|[|E-V<around|(|r|)>|]>=-<frac|1|Y>*<around*|[|<frac|1|sin \<theta\>>*<frac|\<partial\>|\<partial\>*\<theta\>><space|-0.17em><around*|(|sin \<theta\><frac|\<partial\>*Y|\<partial\>*\<theta\>>|)>+<frac|1|sin<rsup|2> \<theta\>>*<frac|\<partial\><rsup|2>*Y|\<partial\>*\<phi\><rsup|2>>|]>.
  </equation*>

  The left-hand side depends only on <math|r>, the right-hand side only on <math|\<theta\>> and <math|\<phi\>>; both must therefore equal a common constant, which we call <math|\<lambda\>>. Thus we obtain the <with|font-series|bold|radial equation>

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:radial1><frac|<dif>|<dif>r>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|r<rsup|2>*<frac|<dif>R|<dif>r>|)>+<frac|2*\<mu\>*r<rsup|2>|\<hbar\><rsup|2>>*<around*|[|E-V<around|(|r|)>|]>*R-\<lambda\>*R=0,
  </equation>

  and the <with|font-series|bold|angular equation>

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:angular1><frac|1|sin \<theta\>>*<frac|\<partial\>|\<partial\>*\<theta\>><space|-0.17em><around*|(|sin \<theta\><frac|\<partial\>*Y|\<partial\>*\<theta\>>|)>+<frac|1|sin<rsup|2> \<theta\>>*<frac|\<partial\><rsup|2>*Y|\<partial\>*\<phi\><rsup|2>>+\<lambda\>*Y=0.
  </equation>

  <section|Solution of the angular equation>

  The angular equation is further separated by writing <math|Y<around|(|\<theta\>,\<phi\>|)>=\<Theta\><around|(|\<theta\>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<Phi\><around|(|\<phi\>|)>>. Substituting and multiplying by <math|sin<rsup|2> \<theta\>/<around|(|\<Theta\>*\<Phi\>|)>> gives

  <\equation*>
    <frac|sin \<theta\>|\<Theta\>>*<frac|<dif>|<dif>\<theta\>><space|-0.17em><around*|(|sin \<theta\><frac|<dif>\<Theta\>|<dif>\<theta\>>|)>+\<lambda\>*sin<rsup|2> \<theta\>=-<frac|1|\<Phi\>>*<frac|<dif><rsup|2>\<Phi\>|<dif>\<phi\><rsup|2>>.
  </equation*>

  Again both sides must equal a constant, which we call <math|m<rsup|2>>. Hence

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<frac|<dif><rsup|2>\<Phi\>|<dif>\<phi\><rsup|2>>+m<rsup|2>*\<Phi\>>|<cell|=0,<eq-number><label|eq:phi>>>|<row|<cell|<frac|1|sin \<theta\>>*<frac|<dif>|<dif>\<theta\>><space|-0.17em><around*|(|sin \<theta\><frac|<dif>\<Theta\>|<dif>\<theta\>>|)>+<around*|(|\<lambda\>-<frac|m<rsup|2>|sin<rsup|2> \<theta\>>|)>*\<Theta\>>|<cell|=0.<eq-number><label|eq:theta>>>>>
  </align>

  <subsection|Azimuthal equation>

  The solution of (<reference|eq:phi>) is

  <\equation*>
    \<Phi\><around|(|\<phi\>|)>=A<space|0.17em><me><rsup|<imag>m*\<phi\>>+B<space|0.17em><me><rsup|-<imag>m*\<phi\>>.
  </equation*>

  The requirement that <math|\<Phi\>> be single-valued, <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<phi\>+2*\<pi\>|)>=\<Phi\><around|(|\<phi\>|)>>, forces <math|m> to be an integer:

  <\equation>
    <boxed|m=0,<space|0.17em>\<pm\>1,<space|0.17em>\<pm\>2,<space|0.17em>\<ldots\>>
  </equation>

  Choosing the normalisation <math|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|2*\<pi\>><around|\||\<Phi\>|\|><rsup|2><dif>\<phi\>=1> we write

  <\equation>
    \<Phi\><rsub|m><around|(|\<phi\>|)>=<frac|1|<sqrt|2*\<pi\>>><space|0.17em><me><rsup|<imag>m*\<phi\>>,<space|2em>m\<in\>\<bbb-Z\>.
  </equation>

  <subsection|Polar equation>

  Set <math|x=cos \<theta\>> (<math|-1\<le\>x\<le\>1>) and <math|P<around|(|x|)>=\<Theta\><around|(|\<theta\>|)>>. Equation (<reference|eq:theta>) transforms into the <with|font-series|bold|associated Legendre equation>

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:Legendre><frac|<dif>|<dif>x>*<space|-0.17em><around*|[|<around|(|1-x<rsup|2>|)>*<frac|<dif>P|<dif>x>|]>+<around*|[|\<lambda\>-<frac|m<rsup|2>|1-x<rsup|2>>|]>*P=0.
  </equation>

  For <math|m=0> it reduces to the ordinary Legendre equation. The requirement that <math|P<around|(|x|)>> be finite at the poles <math|x=\<pm\>1> restricts <math|\<lambda\>> to

  <\equation>
    <boxed|\<lambda\>=l*<around|(|l+1|)>,<space|2em>l=0,1,2,\<ldots\>>
  </equation>

  and for a given <math|l> the allowed values of <math|m> are

  <\equation>
    m=-l,<space|0.17em>-l+1,\<ldots\>,<space|0.17em>l-1,<space|0.17em>l.
  </equation>

  The physically acceptable solutions are the associated Legendre functions

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|l><rsup|m><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|1-x<rsup|2>|)><rsup|<around|\||m|\|>/2>*<space|0.17em><frac|<dif><rsup|<around|\||m|\|>>|<dif>x<rsup|<around|\||m|\|>>>*P<rsub|l><around|(|x|)>,<space|2em>P<rsub|l><around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|2<rsup|l>*l!>*<frac|<dif><rsup|l>|<dif>x<rsup|l>>*<around|(|x<rsup|2>-1|)><rsup|l>.
  </equation*>

  Normalised together with <math|\<Phi\><rsub|m>>, they form the <with|font-series|bold|spherical harmonics>

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:Ylm><boxed|Y<rsub|l><rsup|m><around|(|\<theta\>,\<phi\>|)>=<around|(|-1|)><rsup|m><sqrt|<frac|<around|(|2*l+1|)>|4*\<pi\>>*<space|0.17em><frac|<around|(|l-m|)>!|<around|(|l+m|)>!>>*<space|0.17em>P<rsub|l><rsup|m><around|(|cos \<theta\>|)><space|0.17em><me><rsup|<imag>m*\<phi\>>,>
  </equation>

  where the Condon–Shortley phase convention is used.

  <section|The radial equation>

  Insert <math|\<lambda\>=l*<around|(|l+1|)>> and <math|V<around|(|r|)>=-e<rsup|2>/<around|(|4*\<pi\>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*r|)>> into (<reference|eq:radial1>):

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:radial2>-<frac|\<hbar\><rsup|2>|2*\<mu\>>*<frac|1|r<rsup|2>>*<frac|<dif>|<dif>r>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|r<rsup|2>*<frac|<dif>R|<dif>r>|)>+<around*|[|<frac|\<hbar\><rsup|2>*l*<around|(|l+1|)>|2*\<mu\>*r<rsup|2>>-<frac|e<rsup|2>|4*\<pi\>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*r>|]>*R=E*R.
  </equation>

  It is convenient to introduce the function <math|u<around|(|r|)>=r*R<around|(|r|)>>. Then <math|R=u/r>, and after simplification we obtain a one-dimensional Schr"odinger equation with an effective potential:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:radial_u>-<frac|\<hbar\><rsup|2>|2*\<mu\>>*<frac|<dif><rsup|2>u|<dif>r<rsup|2>>+<wide*|<around*|[|<frac|\<hbar\><rsup|2>*l*<around|(|l+1|)>|2*\<mu\>*r<rsup|2>>-<frac|e<rsup|2>|4*\<pi\>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*r>|]>|\<wide-underbrace\>><rsub|V<rsub|eff><around|(|r|)>>u=E*u.
  </equation>

  We are interested in bound states, <math|E\<less\>0>. Define the positive quantity

  <\equation>
    \<kappa\>=<frac|<sqrt|-2*\<mu\>*E>|\<hbar\>>*<space|2em><around|(|E\<less\>0|)>.
  </equation>

  Change to the dimensionless variable

  <\equation>
    \<rho\>=2*\<kappa\>*r,<space|2em><frac|<dif>|<dif>r>=2*\<kappa\>*<frac|<dif>|<dif>\<rho\>>.
  </equation>

  Equation (<reference|eq:radial_u>) becomes

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:radial_rho><frac|<dif><rsup|2>u|<dif>\<rho\><rsup|2>>=<around*|[|<frac|1|4>-<frac|\<lambda\><rsub|0>|\<rho\>>+<frac|l*<around|(|l+1|)>|\<rho\><rsup|2>>|]>*u,
  </equation>

  where we introduced the parameter

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:lambda0>\<lambda\><rsub|0>=<frac|\<mu\>*e<rsup|2>|4*\<pi\>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*\<hbar\><rsup|2>*\<kappa\>>.
  </equation>

  <subsection|Asymptotic behaviour>

  For large <math|\<rho\>>, (<reference|eq:radial_rho>) tends to <math|<dif><rsup|2>u/<dif>\<rho\><rsup|2>\<approx\>u/4>, so

  <\equation*>
    u<around|(|\<rho\>|)>\<sim\>A<space|0.17em><me><rsup|\<rho\>/2>+B<space|0.17em><me><rsup|-\<rho\>/2>.
  </equation*>

  Normalisability requires <math|A=0>, hence

  <\equation>
    u<around|(|\<rho\>|)>\<sim\><me><rsup|-\<rho\>/2><space|1em><text|as >\<rho\>\<to\>\<infty\>.
  </equation>

  For small <math|\<rho\>>, the centrifugal term dominates, <math|<dif><rsup|2>u/<dif>\<rho\><rsup|2>\<approx\>l*<around|(|l+1|)>*u/\<rho\><rsup|2>>. The power-law ansatz <math|u\<sim\>\<rho\><rsup|s>> gives <math|s*<around|(|s-1|)>=l*<around|(|l+1|)>>, i.e.<nbsp><math|s=l+1> or <math|s=-l>. Regularity at the origin selects

  <\equation>
    u<around|(|\<rho\>|)>\<sim\>\<rho\><rsup|l+1>*<space|1em><text|as >\<rho\>\<to\>0.
  </equation>

  Therefore we factor out the asymptotic behaviour:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:u_form>u<around|(|\<rho\>|)>=\<rho\><rsup|l+1><space|0.17em><me><rsup|-\<rho\>/2><space|0.17em>v<around|(|\<rho\>|)>.
  </equation>

  <subsection|Series solution – confluent hypergeometric equation>

  Substituting (<reference|eq:u_form>) into (<reference|eq:radial_rho>) yields, after straightforward algebra,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:confluent>\<rho\><frac|<dif><rsup|2>v|<dif>\<rho\><rsup|2>>+<around|(|2*l+2-\<rho\>|)>*<frac|<dif>v|<dif>\<rho\>>-<around|(|l+1-\<lambda\><rsub|0>|)>*v=0.
  </equation>

  This is the <with|font-series|bold|confluent hypergeometric equation> (Kummer's equation) <math|z*y<rprime|''>+<around|(|c-z|)>*y<rprime|'>-a*y=0> with

  <\equation*>
    z=\<rho\>,<space|1em>c=2*l+2,<space|1em>a=l+1-\<lambda\><rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  We seek a series solution\ 

  <\equation*>
    v<around|(|\<rho\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k=0><rsup|\<infty\>>a<rsub|k>*<space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsup|k>.
  </equation*>

  Inserting the series and equating powers of <math|\<rho\>> gives the recurrence relation

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:recurrence>a<rsub|k+1>=<frac|k+l+1-\<lambda\><rsub|0>|<around|(|k+1|)>*<around|(|k+2*l+2|)>>*<space|0.17em>a<rsub|k>.
  </equation>

  For large <math|k>, <math|a<rsub|k+1>/a<rsub|k>\<sim\>1/k>, which would produce <math|v<around|(|\<rho\>|)>\<sim\><me><rsup|\<rho\>>> and make <math|u<around|(|\<rho\>|)>\<sim\>\<rho\><rsup|l+1><me><rsup|\<rho\>/2>>, destroying normalisability. Therefore the series must terminate, i.e.<nbsp>there exists a non-negative integer <math|n<rsub|r>> such that <math|a<rsub|n<rsub|r>+1>=0> while <math|a<rsub|n<rsub|r>>\<neq\>0>. From (<reference|eq:recurrence>) this requires

  <\equation>
    n<rsub|r>+l+1-\<lambda\><rsub|0>=0,<space|2em>n<rsub|r>=0,1,2,\<ldots\>
  </equation>

  Define the <with|font-series|bold|principal quantum number>

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:n_def><boxed|n\<equiv\>\<lambda\><rsub|0>=n<rsub|r>+l+1.>
  </equation>

  Since <math|n<rsub|r>\<ge\>0>, we have <math|n\<ge\>l+1>, i.e.<nbsp>for a given <math|n>,

  <\equation>
    l=0,1,2,\<ldots\>,n-1.
  </equation>

  The integer <math|n> takes values <math|n=1,2,3,\<ldots\>>

  <subsection|Energy quantization>

  From <math|\<lambda\><rsub|0>=n> and definition (<reference|eq:lambda0>) we find <math|\<kappa\>>. Using <math|\<kappa\>=<sqrt|-2*\<mu\>*E>/\<hbar\>>,

  <\equation*>
    <frac|\<mu\>*e<rsup|2>|4*\<pi\>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*\<hbar\><rsup|2>*\<kappa\>>=n<space|1em>\<Longrightarrow\><space|1em>\<kappa\>=<frac|\<mu\>*e<rsup|2>|4*\<pi\>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*\<hbar\><rsup|2>*n>.
  </equation*>

  Hence the bound-state energies are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:En><boxed|E<rsub|n>=-<frac|\<mu\>*e<rsup|4>|2*<around|(|4*\<pi\>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>|)><rsup|2>*\<hbar\><rsup|2>>*<space|0.17em><frac|1|n<rsup|2>>=-R<rsub|H>*<space|0.17em><frac|1|n<rsup|2>>,>
  </equation>

  where <math|R<rsub|H>=<dfrac|\<mu\>*e<rsup|4>|2*<around|(|4*\<pi\>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>|)><rsup|2>*\<hbar\><rsup|2>>> is the Rydberg constant. For hydrogen, <math|\<mu\>\<approx\>m<rsub|e>> and <math|R<rsub|H>\<approx\>13.6<space|0.27em>>eV.

  <subsection|Radial wavefunctions>

  The terminated series <math|v<around|(|\<rho\>|)>> is a polynomial of degree <math|n<rsub|r>=n-l-1>. Apart from normalisation, it is the associated Laguerre polynomial <math|L<rsub|n-l-1><rsup|<around|(|2*l+1|)>><around|(|\<rho\>|)>>. Reinstating <math|r>, the normalised radial function is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:Rnl><boxed|R<rsub|n*l><around|(|r|)>=<sqrt|<around*|(|<frac|2|n*a<rsub|0>>|)><rsup|3><frac|<around|(|n-l-1|)>!|2*n*<space|0.17em><around|(|n+l|)>!>><space|0.27em><around*|(|<frac|2*r|n*a<rsub|0>>|)><rsup|l><me><rsup|-r/<around|(|n*a<rsub|0>|)>><space|0.17em>L<rsub|n-l-1><rsup|<space|0.17em>2*l+1><space|-0.17em><around*|(|<frac|2*r|n*a<rsub|0>>|)>,>
  </equation>

  where

  <\equation*>
    a<rsub|0>=<frac|4*\<pi\>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|0>*\<hbar\><rsup|2>|\<mu\>*e<rsup|2>>
  </equation*>

  is the <with|font-series|bold|Bohr radius> (\<approx\> <math|0.529<space|0.17em>>Å). The associated Laguerre polynomials are defined by the Rodrigues formula\ 

  <\equation*>
    L<rsub|q><rsup|p><around|(|x|)>=<frac|<me><rsup|x>x<rsup|-p>|q!>*<frac|<dif><rsup|q>|<dif>x<rsup|q>><around*|(|<me><rsup|-x>x<rsup|p+q>|)>.
  </equation*>

  <section|Complete wavefunctions>

  Collecting all factors, the stationary states of the hydrogen atom are labelled by the three quantum numbers <math|n,l,m>:

  <\equation>
    <boxed|\<psi\><rsub|n*l*m><around|(|r,\<theta\>,\<phi\>|)>=R<rsub|n*l><around|(|r|)>*<space|0.17em>Y<rsub|l><rsup|m><around|(|\<theta\>,\<phi\>|)>,>
  </equation>

  with

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|n>|<cell|=1,2,3,\<ldots\>,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|l>|<cell|=0,1,\<ldots\>,n-1,<eq-number>>>|<row|<cell|m>|<cell|=-l,-l+1,\<ldots\>,l.<eq-number>>>>>
  </align>

  The ground state (<math|n=1>, <math|l=0>, <math|m=0>) is

  <\equation*>
    \<psi\><rsub|100><around|(|r,\<theta\>,\<phi\>|)>=<frac|1|<sqrt|\<pi\>*a<rsub|0><rsup|3>>><space|0.17em><me><rsup|-r/a<rsub|0>>,<space|2em>E<rsub|1>=-13.6<space|0.27em><text|eV>.
  </equation*>

  All wavefunctions are orthogonal and form a complete set:\ 

  <\equation*>
    <big|int>\<psi\><rsub|n*l*m><rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|<math-bf|r>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<psi\><rsub|n<rprime|'>*l<rprime|'>*m<rprime|'>><around|(|<math-bf|r>|)><space|0.27em><dif><rsup|3>r=\<delta\><rsub|n*n<rprime|'>>*\<delta\><rsub|l*l<rprime|'>>*\<delta\><rsub|m*m<rprime|'>>.
  </equation*>

  <section*|Summary of the key steps>

  <\enumerate>
    <item>Separation of centre-of-mass motion reduces the problem to a single particle of reduced mass <math|\<mu\>> in a Coulomb potential.

    <item>In spherical coordinates the Schrödinger equation separates into radial and angular parts with separation constant <math|\<lambda\>=l*<around|(|l+1|)>>.

    <item>The azimuthal equation gives <math|\<Phi\><rsub|m><around|(|\<phi\>|)>=<me><rsup|<imag>m*\<phi\>>/<sqrt|2*\<pi\>>> with integer <math|m>.

    <item>The polar equation yields the associated Legendre functions and restricts <math|l\<ge\><around|\||m|\|>>.

    <item>The radial equation is transformed into a confluent hypergeometric equation. The requirement that the series terminates quantises the energy and introduces the principal quantum number <math|n=n<rsub|r>+l+1>.

    <item>The radial solutions are expressed in terms of associated Laguerre polynomials; the total wavefunction is a product of radial and angular parts.
  </enumerate>
</body>

